2. THE CASE HAS NO AUTHORED TEXT -- what was designed, and from what
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Read from ck.db on tb277 before dispatch: AWPTCM-T33403 has `has_objective = 0` and ONE
step whose text is the 53-character empty-JSON placeholder. There is no objective, no
step text and no expected result in the database. Folder
"/New Platform Test (MASTER)/New Platform Template/Management", status Draft,
script_type STEP_BY_STEP, label New_Platform_Management.

Designed from the three sources STANDING-ORDERS allows, in this order:

  1. THE TITLE: "Management_Operation - Scripting". One word, and it does not say what a
     script IS on this platform. Establishing that was the first job of the case.
  2. THE X960 TPS ROW. The bracketed "(269)" in the title is NOT the TPS row number --
     on this suite the title brackets are offset +2. The row for this case is
     **267, "Scripting", Supported = Yes**. A feature missing on the DUT would therefore
     be a FINDING, not a bench limit.
  3. THE DUT'S OWN CLI SURFACE, read read-only with tools/qmark.py before designing --
     section 4 below.

OBJECTIVE AS DESIGNED
  Establish what "scripting" is on the AT-x960-56YSQ, and then prove that a script is
  genuinely EXECUTED rather than merely accepted: that a script file on the device's flash
  can be run on demand and leaves an artefact only a running script could have made; that
  the same script can be run by a TRIGGER without anyone typing anything; that a trigger
  does not fire on something it should not match; that script, trigger and artefacts
  survive a cold boot and still work afterwards; and that all of it removes cleanly,
  proved by re-running the stimulus and getting nothing.

DESIGNED STEPS, WRITTEN DOWN BEFORE THEY WERE RUN
  STEP 0  Baseline: no triggers, no script file on flash, both working ports bare.
  STEP 1  Establish the surface, read-only, with qmark (no CR, nothing executed).
  STEP 2  Put a real script on the device's flash and show its contents.
  STEP 3  ACTIVATE it by hand. The named port must gain the artefact; a port the script
          does NOT name must stay bare, read in the same run (the control).
  STEP 4  Make a TRIGGER run a second script with nobody typing the script's commands.
          The trigger's condition is a log-message regular expression, so the control
          is built in: a NEAR-MISS string that the regex must reject, sent first, in the
          same run as the string that must match.
  STEP 5  Persistence: write + reload. Trigger, script file and artefacts must survive,
          and the trigger must still FIRE afterwards -- not merely still be configured.
  STEP 6  Removal: trigger gone, script files gone from flash, artefacts gone from
          running- and startup-config, and the matching stimulus re-sent to show it now
          does nothing.

