4. STEP 0 AND STEP 1 -- THE BASELINE, AND WHAT "SCRIPTING" IS ON THIS PLATFORM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4a. Baseline, before anything was changed

  awplus# show trigger
  TR# Type & Details     Description          Ac Te Tr Repeat     #Scr Days/Date
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  % No triggers currently defined
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ⚠ That `% ` line is from a SUCCESSFUL show. It is informational, not an error, and a
    stop-on-error driver aborts on it. Every `show` in this case therefore went through
    tools/ckcon.py, never tools/cfg.py (platforms/x960.md §7F.6 says exactly this).

  awplus# show running-config | include trigger      -> nothing
  awplus# dir flash:                                 -> no .scp file of any kind
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.20   -> switchport / switchport mode access
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21   -> switchport / switchport mode access
  awplus# show interface port1.0.20 status
  port1.0.20  -   notconnect   1  auto  auto  not present

  The two working ports are unused access ports with NO description and no module in the
  cage ("not present"). port1.0.22 is the same and is held back as a second control. None
  of the ports that were carrying links (1.0.2, 1.0.4, 1.0.8, 1.0.10, 1.0.14, 1.0.16,
  1.0.48) was touched at any point in this case.

4b. 🔴 THERE IS NO `show script` COMMAND -- said in exactly those words

  awplus# show script
  ^
  % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.

  No `show script` CLI command exists on this platform. (The triage note predicted the
  wording "% Unrecognized command"; what the device actually prints is the caret and
  "% Invalid input detected at '^' marker." The conclusion is the same, and the exact
  text is recorded here because that is what the next person will see.) A script file is
  listed by `dir flash:` and its contents are read with `start-shell`, not by any
  script-specific show command.

4c. The surface, read with qmark (types `<prefix> ?` with NO carriage return, then clears
    the line with Ctrl-U, so nothing is executed; its self-test proved that first)

  awplus# activate ?
    SCRIPT      Script to activate
    background  Activate script in background
  awplus# activate flash ?
    ARGUMENT  Arguments for the script
    <cr>

  awplus(config)# trigger 1
  awplus(config-trigger)# type ?
    atmf  cpu  env-sensor  interface  linkmon-probe  log  main-pse  memory  periodic
    ping-poll  pse-port  reboot  stack  time  usb                      (fifteen types)
  awplus(config-trigger)# type interface ?
    IFNAME  Interface name
  awplus(config-trigger)# type interface port1.0.20 ?
    any   Activate when any interface link status event occurs
    down  Activate when the interface closes
    up    Activate when the interface becomes operational
    <cr>
  awplus(config-trigger)# type log ?
    LINE  Log filter regular expression
  awplus(config-trigger)# type log 5 ?
    LINE  Log filter regular expression
    <cr>
  awplus(config-trigger)# script ?
    <1-5>  The list position of the script
  awplus(config-trigger)# script 1 ?
    FILE  The file containing the script

  Entering `trigger 1` and leaving without setting a `type` creates nothing, so this
  probe left no state behind -- confirmed afterwards by `show trigger` still printing
  `% No triggers currently defined`.

>>> SO: "SCRIPTING" ON THIS PLATFORM IS A `.scp` FILE ON THE DEVICE'S FLASH, run either
>>> by the exec command `activate <file>` or automatically by a `trigger`, which can take
>>> up to five script files in an ordered list. There is no scripting LANGUAGE: the file
>>> holds AlliedWare Plus CLI commands and they are executed as though typed.

>>> DESIGN DECISION, and the reason this case is stronger than the obvious version of it.
>>> The natural trigger for a bench test is `type interface <port> down` with a port
>>> flap. On THIS unit every candidate port is an SFP cage with no module ("not present"),
>>> so an interface event is not reliably producible without disturbing a port that is
>>> carrying a link -- and those are the campaign's own legs and the Test Engineer's
>>> vlan 60 on the RSTP root port. `type log <regex>` needs no cable at all: the DUT logs
>>> every CLI command it is given, so a command carrying a chosen string IS the stimulus,
>>> produced on demand and timed to the second. It also makes the control exact, as
>>> STEP 4 shows.

