6. STEP 3 -- ACTIVATE IT BY HAND, WITH A CONTROL PORT IN THE SAME RUN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  BEFORE, both ports bare:
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.20
  !
  interface port1.0.20
   switchport
   switchport mode access
  !
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21      -> identical, no description

  awplus# activate flash:/ckt3a.scp
  AlliedWare Plus (TM) 0.0.0 10/18/26 23:18:21
  enable
  configure terminal
  Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
  interface port1.0.20
  description CKT33403-ACTIVATE-RAN
  end
  exit

  The device echoes a fresh CLI banner and then every line of the file in order -- the
  script is run as a CLI session of its own, not interpreted by some other engine.

  AFTER:
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.20
  !
  interface port1.0.20
   description CKT33403-ACTIVATE-RAN          <- the artefact, from the script
   switchport
   switchport mode access
  !
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21
  !
  interface port1.0.21
   switchport
   switchport mode access                     <- CONTROL: untouched
  !
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.22
  !
  interface port1.0.22
   switchport
   switchport mode access                     <- second CONTROL: untouched

  STEP 3 VERDICT: PASS -- a script file on flash is EXECUTED by `activate`, and it changed
  exactly the one port it names. Two ports the script does not name were read in the same
  run and are unchanged, so the result is a measurement and not a coincidence.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. STEP 4 -- A TRIGGER RUNS THE SECOND SCRIPT, WITH A NEAR-MISS CONTROL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7a. The trigger, and why its regex is written the way it is

  awplus# configure terminal
  awplus(config)# trigger 1
  awplus(config-trigger)# type log CK[0-9]33403FIRE
  awplus(config-trigger)# script 1 flash:/ckt3b.scp
  awplus(config-trigger)# end

  🔑 THE REGEX IS DELIBERATELY ONE THAT DOES NOT MATCH ITS OWN CONFIGURATION LINE. The
  DUT logs every command typed, so `type log <pattern>` would normally log a line
  CONTAINING the pattern and fire the trigger at the moment it was configured, making
  every later result ambiguous. `CK[0-9]33403FIRE` requires a DIGIT after "CK"; the
  configuration line contains the literal text "CK[0-9]33403FIRE", whose next character
  is "[", not a digit. So it cannot match itself. That is confirmed below, and it also
  gives the case an exact control for free.

  awplus# show running-config | include trigger
  trigger 1
     ⚠ NOTE: the grep shows the trigger EXISTS and nothing about what it does -- the
       sub-mode lines do not contain the word "trigger". `show trigger <n>` is the read
       that matters (platforms/x960.md §7F.6).

  awplus# show trigger 1
  Trigger Configuration Details
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  Trigger ..................... 1
  Description ................. <no description>
  Type and details ............ Log (CK[0-9]33403FIRE)
  Days ........................ smtwtfs
  After ....................... 00:00:00
  Before ...................... 23:59:59
  Active ...................... Yes
  Test ........................ No
  Trap ........................ Yes
  Repeat ...................... Continuous
  Modified .................... Thu Oct 22 01:08:37 2026
  Number of activations ....... 0            <- it did NOT fire on its own config line
  Last activation ............. not activated
  Number of scripts ........... 1
    1. flash:/ckt3b.scp
    2. <not configured>
    3. <not configured>
    4. <not configured>
    5. <not configured>
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21   -> still bare, no description

7b. THE CONTROL FIRST -- a near-miss that the regex must reject

  awplus# show clock | include CKX33403FIRE        <- "X" is not a digit
  (8 s later)
  awplus# show trigger 1
  Number of activations ....... 0
  Last activation ............. not activated
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21  -> still bare

  A log message was certainly produced -- the DUT logs every command -- and it differs
  from a matching one by a single character. The trigger did not fire and the script did
  not run.

7c. THE MATCHING STRING

  awplus# show clock | include CK733403FIRE        <- "7" is a digit
  (10 s later)
  awplus# show trigger 1
  Number of activations ....... 1
  Last activation ............. Thu Oct 22 01:12:30 2026   [01:09:23 on this first run]
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21
  !
  interface port1.0.21
   description CKT33403-TRIGGER-RAN           <- the second script's artefact
   switchport
   switchport mode access
  !
  awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.22  -> still bare (control)

  awplus# show log | include TRIGGER
  2026 Oct 22 01:09:23 user.notice awplus TRIGGER[4679]: Trigger 1 activated
  2026 Oct 22 01:09:23 user.notice awplus IMISH[4689]: [SCRIPT]description CKT33403-TRIGGER-RAN

>>> THE `[SCRIPT]` PREFIX IS THE PROOF. The device's own log distinguishes a command that
>>> came from a script from one typed on a console: a console command is logged as
>>> `[manager@ttyS0]<command>` and this one is logged as `[SCRIPT]<command>`. Nobody typed
>>> `description CKT33403-TRIGGER-RAN`; the trigger ran the file, and the device says so.
>>> Activation and script execution are in the same second as the stimulus (01:09:23).

>>> THIS CLOSES AN OPEN ITEM IN THE PLATFORM FILE. platforms/x960.md §7F.8 records that a
>>> trigger's action list was only ever proved to run by pointing `script 1` at a
>>> DELIBERATELY NON-EXISTENT file and reading the resulting "Unable to activate script
>>> ... (File not found)" error, and states: "Running a real script still needs a .scp on
>>> the device's flash, which needs a root write to the box's /tftproot
>>> (STANDING-ORDERS §4) -- still unmeasured, as it was after T44181." It is measured now,
>>> and it needed no write to the box at all.

  STEP 4 VERDICT: PASS -- a trigger executes a real script with nobody typing its
  commands; the condition is a genuine regular expression, shown by a one-character
  near-miss that was rejected in the same run; and the artefact appeared on the one port
  the script names while two others stayed bare.

