    log (filtered) .. 35 new non-echo lines, all kern.* start-up text of that boot
    log tail 150 .... 150 lines, 109 echoes of our own commands; other lines by signature:
          4 x 01:11:11 syslog.notice syslog-ng[543]: Configuration reload request received
          4 x 01:11:11 syslog.notice syslog-ng[543]: Configuration reload finished
          3 x 01:11:16 daemon.notice root: DHCP Client: Do PREINIT6
          1 x 01:11:11 user.notice NSM[943]: port1.0.2 updated vlans
          1 x 01:11:11 user.notice NSM[943]: port1.0.10 port mode access updated VLAN
          1 x 01:11:11 user.notice NSM[943]: port1.0.48 port mode access updated VLAN
    !! flagged ...... 35 line(s) matching kernel|exception|crash|restart|Unexpected
    cpu ............. 0% -> 0%  (1 s busy average)
    memory .......... free 7456420 -> 7454748 kB (-1672)
    VERDICT ......... !! DIRTY

  ADJUDICATION BY THE TESTER: **CLEAN**. The reasoning in full:

  1. THE NEW REBOOT ENTRY IS THIS CASE'S OWN RELOAD, matched to the second. The entry is
     `2026-10-22 01:10:14  Expected  CLI(user request)`. The DUT runs UTC and the box
     displays NZDT, 13 h ahead, so 01:10:14 UTC is 14:10:14 NZDT -- and the reload
     transcript in section 8 reads `14:10:14 >>> reload`. The same second. Its category is
     `Expected ... CLI(user request)`, not `Unexpected System reboot`. It is STEP 5 of the
     design and was explicitly permitted by the Test Engineer for this campaign.
  2. THE 35 FLAGGED LINES ARE THAT BOOT'S ORDINARY START-UP TEXT, all stamped 01:11:0x,
     all `kern.notice` or `kern.warning`, and word for word the same set the BEFORE sample
     carries from T33402's reload. They are flagged only because the §8 filter matches the
     literal word `kernel`, which every `kern.*` line contains. No crash, no Oops, no
     panic, no unrequested daemon restart.
  3. EXCEPTION LOG `None -> None`.
  4. CPU 0% in both samples. Memory free moved by 1672 kB of 8092132 kB, which is 0.02% --
     no step change, and one case cannot show a leak; this log does not claim one.
  5. THE REMAINING TAIL LINES ARE THE EXPECTED WORK OF A REBOOT: syslog-ng reloading its
     configuration, the DHCP client starting, and NSM re-applying the VLAN membership of
     port1.0.2, port1.0.10 and port1.0.48 -- the Test Engineer's vlan 60 trunk and the two
     campaign legs, coming back exactly as they should. Nothing names a trigger or a
     script, which is itself worth noting: the trigger that had been removed left nothing
     behind in the log.

  So the run is **CLEAN, not confounded**, and nothing is reported to the sentinel as a
  health finding.

