13. SUMMARY OF STEP VERDICTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  STEP 0  baseline: no triggers, no .scp on flash, both working ports bare .... baseline
  STEP 1  the surface established read-only; **no `show script` command exists** .. PASS
  STEP 2  two real scripts placed on the device's flash and read back ........... PASS
  STEP 3  `activate flash:/ckt3a.scp` EXECUTED the file -- the named port gained
          the artefact, two unnamed ports stayed bare in the same run .......... PASS
  STEP 4  a TRIGGER ran the second script with nobody typing its commands;
          `TRIGGER[...]: Trigger 1 activated` + `IMISH[...]: [SCRIPT]<command>`;
          a one-character near-miss was REJECTED in the same run .............. PASS
  STEP 5  script file, trigger and artefacts survive a cold boot, and the trigger
          still FIRES and still rejects the near-miss afterwards .............. PASS
  STEP 6  removal complete in running-config, startup-config, `show trigger`,
          on flash, and against the re-sent live stimulus ..................... PASS

  FINDINGS AND OBSERVATIONS RAISED
    (1) **No `show script` CLI command exists on this platform** -- in exactly those
        words. `show script` returns the caret and "% Invalid input detected at '^'
        marker." A script is listed by `dir flash:` and read with `start-shell`.
        "Scripting" here is a `.scp` file of ordinary CLI commands on the device's flash,
        run by the exec command `activate <file>` or by a `trigger` (up to five files in
        an ordered list). There is no scripting language.
    (2) **platforms/x960.md §7F.8's open item is now CLOSED.** That note says a trigger's
        action list had only ever been proved to run by pointing it at a non-existent file
        and reading the error, and that running a REAL script "needs a root write to the
        box's /tftproot (STANDING-ORDERS §4) -- still unmeasured". A real script has now
        been run, twice, by a trigger, and it needed no write to the testbox at all.
    (3) The device's log distinguishes a script's commands from a console's:
        `[SCRIPT]<command>` versus `[manager@ttyS0]<command>`. That is the cheapest
        available proof that a command came from a script.
    (4) `trigger <n>` entered and left without a `type` creates nothing, so a read-only
        help probe of the sub-mode is safe (confirmed again here).
    (5) `show running-config | include trigger` prints only `trigger 1` and nothing about
        what the trigger does -- the sub-mode lines do not contain the word. Use
        `show trigger <n>`.
    (6) A trigger's `Number of activations` is a RUNTIME counter and resets to 0 at
        reboot, while the trigger itself persists. Do not read the reset as a loss of
        configuration.
    (7) `dir flash: | include <x>` is REJECTED -- `dir` takes no pipe filter here.
    (8) `show trigger` with no triggers prints `% No triggers currently defined`, a `% `
        line from a SUCCESSFUL show. Run it through ckcon.py, never cfg.py.
    (9) METHOD, reusable: when a trigger's condition is `type log <regex>`, write a regex
        that cannot match its own configuration line (here `CK[0-9]33403FIRE`, since the
        configuration line has "[" where the regex needs a digit). Otherwise the trigger
        fires the moment it is configured -- the DUT logs every command typed -- and every
        later result is ambiguous. The same trick hands you an exact control for free: a
        one-character near-miss that must be rejected.

RUN LABEL: clean. (The health tool read DIRTY; adjudicated CLEAN in section 10 with the
reasoning in full -- the single new reboot entry is this case's own permitted reload,
matched to the second, and every flagged line is that boot's ordinary kern.* start-up
text.)

VERDICT: PASS -- "scripting" on the AT-x960-56YSQ is a `.scp` file of CLI commands on the
device's flash, run by `activate <file>` or by a trigger, and there is no `show script`
command. A real script was executed both ways and proved by an artefact only a running
script could have made, with ports the script does not name read in the same run and
unchanged. The trigger's log-regular-expression condition was shown to be a real regex by
a one-character near-miss that it rejected in the same run as the string it accepted, and
the device's own `[SCRIPT]` log prefix shows the command came from the file and not from a
console. Script, trigger and artefacts survive a cold boot and the trigger still fires
afterwards. Everything removes cleanly, proved by re-sending the stimulus and getting
nothing. This case also closes the open item recorded in platforms/x960.md §7F.8.
